-- Q1011: Trusts and Estates [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Three separate trusts are optimal because each trust claims a distribution deduction and income is taxed at individual rates while preserving asset protection","A single trust is best because consolidating administration reduces costs and the $600,000 is taxed identically regardless of how many trusts are created","Direct distribution to the children is always preferable because it avoids trust-level taxation entirely and the children''s lower brackets minimize total tax","Three separate trusts provide no benefit because the IRS treats related trusts with the same grantor as a single taxpayer under consolidation rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Three trusts, each distributing $200,000, claim distribution deductions under Section 661, passing income to beneficiaries at their individual graduated rates rather than the trust''s compressed brackets (37% begins at $15,200). Separate trusts also provide asset protection against creditors and divorcing spouses that direct distribution does not.
Wrong (B): A single trust produces the same tax result if it distributes all income, but offers less allocation flexibility and less segregated asset protection per child.
Wrong (C): Direct distribution achieves the same income tax result but sacrifices asset protection, which is a stated family goal.
Wrong (D): IRS consolidation rules target substantially identical trusts created primarily for tax avoidance, not trusts with separate beneficiaries and legitimate non-tax purposes.$EXPL$
