-- Q1237: Nontaxable Dispositions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both properties have a two-year replacement period and must meet the ''similar or related in service or use'' functional-use test for replacement property","The warehouse must be replaced within two years under the functional-use test, while the condemned lot gets three years and the broader like-kind standard under Section 1033(g)","Both properties qualify for a three-year replacement period and the broader like-kind standard because they were destroyed in a federally declared disaster","The warehouse has a four-year replacement period due to the disaster declaration, while the condemned lot has a two-year period under the standard rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1033, casualties require replacement with property ''similar or related in service or use'' (the narrow functional-use test) within two years. A warehouse replaced with retail space would likely fail this test. For condemnation of real property, Section 1033(g) applies the broader like-kind standard and a three-year replacement period.
Wrong (A): Condemnation of real property uses the broader like-kind standard under Section 1033(g), not the narrow functional-use test that applies to casualties.
Wrong (C): The disaster provision may extend the replacement period but does not change the replacement standard from functional-use to like-kind for the casualty property.
Wrong (D): The standard casualty replacement period is two years, not four, and the condemned lot receives three years under Section 1033(g), not two.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1237;

-- Q1245: Nontaxable Dispositions [hard/L4]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Use the installment method to defer all gain and spread the tax liability over the payment period","Request a private letter ruling from the IRS before completing the installment sale transaction","Donate the appreciated asset to a qualified charity to avoid recognizing the built-in gain entirely","Elect out of the installment method to recognize the full gain and offset it with existing capital loss carryforwards"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 453(d), a taxpayer may elect out of the installment method, recognizing the entire $200,000 gain in the year of sale. This allows the $150,000 of capital loss carryforwards under Section 1212(b) to offset the gain, resulting in only $50,000 of net taxable gain in the current year.
Wrong (A): Using the installment method defers gain recognition, risking that the capital loss carryforwards expire unused under Section 1212(b)''s five-year limitation.
Wrong (B): A private letter ruling is unnecessary because the election out of the installment method is a statutory right under Section 453(d).
Wrong (C): Donating the asset changes the economic transaction entirely and does not address the strategic use of existing carryforwards.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 1245;

-- Q3921: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The deferral election must be made before the beginning of the taxable year in which the compensation is earned","The plan must be funded through a secular trust that holds assets for the employee''s benefit","The employee must agree to forfeit all deferred amounts upon voluntary termination of employment","The employer must deposit deferred amounts into a segregated escrow account each pay period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 409A, deferral elections for NQDC plans must be made before the beginning of the taxable year in which the services giving rise to the compensation are performed. Failure to meet the timing rules results in immediate income inclusion plus a 20% additional tax and interest.
Wrong (B): NQDC plans are typically unfunded promises to pay; funding through a secular trust would trigger constructive receipt and immediate taxation under Section 402(b).
Wrong (C): Section 409A does not require forfeiture upon termination; it governs the timing of elections and distributions, not vesting conditions.
Wrong (D): Segregated escrow accounts would constitute funded arrangements, causing immediate taxation; NQDC plans must remain unfunded to defer taxation.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3921;

-- Q3932: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["NSOs create an AMT adjustment at exercise while ISOs do not trigger any alternative minimum tax consequences","ISOs have no regular income recognition at exercise if holding periods are met, while NSOs are taxed as ordinary income at exercise","ISOs provide the employer with a compensation deduction at exercise, while NSOs generate no employer deduction","NSOs require a two-year holding period from the grant date, while ISOs have no statutory holding period requirement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 421(a), no regular income tax is recognized upon exercise of an ISO if statutory holding periods are met (two years from grant, one year from exercise). By contrast, under Sec. 83(a), NSO exercise triggers ordinary income equal to the spread between FMV and exercise price.
