UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Property or services provided by an employer that would be deductible as a business expense if the employee had paid for them","An employer-provided vehicle used exclusively for the employee''s personal commuting and errands","Free meals provided for the convenience of the employer to employees working on the business premises","Health insurance premiums paid by the employer and excluded from the employee''s gross income"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 132(d), a working condition fringe benefit is any property or service provided by the employer to the extent the employee could have deducted the cost under Section 162 (trade or business expense) or Section 167 (depreciation) if the employee had paid for it directly.
Wrong (B): A vehicle used exclusively for personal purposes fails the Section 132(d) test because personal commuting is not deductible as a business expense under Section 162.
Wrong (C): Employer-provided meals on business premises are excluded under the de minimis fringe or Section 119 rules, not the working condition fringe benefit provision.
Wrong (D): Employer-paid health insurance is excluded under Section 106, which is a separate exclusion from the working condition fringe benefit under Section 132(d).$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3937;

-- Q3953: Individual Tax Planning: Compensation [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Full funding, vesting, and participation requirements apply under ERISA Title I just as they do for qualified plans","Only the reporting and disclosure requirements of ERISA apply, with exemptions from participation, vesting, and funding rules","No ERISA requirements whatsoever apply to top-hat plans maintained for select management groups","Top-hat plans must satisfy the same fiduciary, funding, and vesting standards as tax-qualified retirement plans"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ERISA Sec. 201(2) and 301(a)(3), top-hat plans (unfunded NQDC plans for a select group of management or highly compensated employees) are exempt from participation, vesting, funding, and fiduciary requirements. However, they remain subject to ERISA''s reporting and disclosure provisions and must file a one-time statement with the Department of Labor.
Wrong (A): Top-hat plans are specifically exempted from funding and vesting requirements; applying full Title I requirements would defeat the purpose of the top-hat exemption.
Wrong (C): Top-hat plans are not entirely exempt from ERISA; they must still comply with reporting and disclosure obligations under Part 1 of Title I.
Wrong (D): Top-hat plans intentionally operate outside the qualified plan framework; requiring identical standards would eliminate the flexibility they are designed to provide.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3953;

-- Q3958: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Salary earned from Felicia''s full-time software engineering position with her employer","Dividends received from a publicly traded stock portfolio held in a brokerage account","Net rental income from a residential property in which the taxpayer does not materially participate","Interest income earned from a savings account held at a commercial bank"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 469, passive income includes income from rental activities (which are per se passive with limited exceptions) and trade or business activities in which the taxpayer does not materially participate. Net rental income qualifies as passive income that can be offset by passive losses.
Wrong (A): Salary is active (earned) income from services performed, not passive income, and cannot be offset by passive activity losses under Section 469.
Wrong (B): Dividends from publicly traded stocks are portfolio income under Section 469, a separate category from passive income that cannot absorb passive losses.
Wrong (D): Interest income from savings accounts is portfolio income, not passive income, and is excluded from the passive activity rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3958;

-- Q3993: Passive Activity and At-Risk Rules [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because spending more than 500 hours on the rental activity satisfies the material participation standard","No, because the taxpayer''s AGI exceeds $75,000 and the $25,000 active participation allowance is fully phased out","Yes, because the taxpayer materially participated in the rental activity by performing 600 hours of rental services","No, the rental remains passive, but the $15,000 loss may be deductible under the $25,000 active participation allowance"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): The taxpayer does not qualify as a real estate professional because more than half of personal services (2,000 hours engineering vs. 600 hours rental) were not in real property trades or businesses under Section 469(c)(7). Rental activities remain per se passive. However, with active participation and AGI of $85,000 (below the $100,000 phase-out start), the full $15,000 loss is deductible under the $25,000 special allowance of Section 469(i).
Wrong (A): The 500-hour test is a material participation standard for non-rental activities; rental activities are per se passive unless the real estate professional exception applies.
Wrong (B): The $25,000 allowance begins phasing out at $100,000 AGI, not $75,000; at $85,000 AGI, the full allowance is still available.
Wrong (C): Material participation in a rental activity does not convert it to nonpassive unless the taxpayer also qualifies as a real estate professional.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3993;

-- Q4002: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The grantor avoids all income tax on the annuity payments received from the GRAT during the trust term","The annuity payments from the GRAT are deductible by the grantor as charitable contributions on the income tax return","The GRAT assets receive a stepped-up basis at the grantor''s death if the grantor dies during the trust term","The remainder interest has a gift tax value of approximately zero while future appreciation passes transfer-tax-free"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A zeroed-out GRAT is structured so the present value of annuity payments approximately equals the assets transferred, making the taxable gift of the remainder interest near zero. If trust assets appreciate faster than the Section 7520 rate, the excess appreciation passes to remainder beneficiaries free of gift and estate tax.
Wrong (A): A GRAT is a grantor trust, so the grantor reports all trust income on the individual return; the annuity payments do not provide income tax avoidance.
Wrong (B): GRAT annuity payments are returns of the grantor''s own assets, not charitable contributions, and generate no income tax deduction.
Wrong (C): If the grantor dies during the GRAT term, the trust assets are generally included in the gross estate under Section 2036, so there is no basis step-up benefit unique to this planning.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4002;

-- Q4008: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both spouses must consent to split all gifts made by either spouse during the entire calendar year","Both spouses must be United States citizens at the time the gifts are made during the year","The gifts subject to splitting must be made only to the spouses'' children or lineal descendants","Gift splitting is available only for individual gifts that exceed the $100,000 reporting threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 2513, gift splitting requires that both spouses consent to split all gifts made by either spouse during the calendar year. Both must be married at the time of the gift and must file Form 709 to make the election. The consent applies to all gifts for the year, not selectively.
Wrong (B): Both spouses must be married but need not be U.S. citizens; however, gifts to a non-citizen spouse may have different annual exclusion limits under Section 2523(i).
Wrong (C): Gift splitting applies to gifts made to any donee, not only children or lineal descendants; the election covers all gifts by either spouse.
Wrong (D): There is no minimum gift amount for gift splitting; the election is available regardless of the size of the individual gifts.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4008;

-- Q4009: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The remainder passing to the children has a reduced gift tax value because the charitable lead interest is subtracted from the total value","The donor receives a current income tax deduction equal to the full $500,000 contributed to the CLAT","The annual charity payments made by the CLAT are deductible by the children as charitable contributions","The trust assets are excluded from the donor''s estate regardless of when the donor dies during or after the term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): A CLAT reduces the taxable gift of the remainder interest by the present value of the charitable lead annuity. The gift tax value of the remainder equals the FMV of assets transferred minus the present value of the charity payments. If trust assets grow faster than the Section 7520 rate, excess appreciation passes to the children transfer-tax-free.
Wrong (B): A nongrantor CLAT provides no current income tax deduction to the donor; a grantor CLAT may allow one, but not for the full amount contributed.
Wrong (C): The charity payments are made by the trust, not the children; the children have no deduction because they are remainder beneficiaries, not donors.
Wrong (D): If the donor retains certain powers or the CLAT is a grantor trust, the assets may be included in the donor''s estate under Sections 2036 or 2038.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4009;
